import{_ as r,E as n,h as i,c as o,b as t,f as s,H as h,a4 as c}from"./chunks/framework.B_fYP6Of.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"A research walkthrough","description":"Follow three fictional harbor reports through source tracing, identity review, publication, an interrupted answer, and portable export.","frontmatter":{"title":"A research walkthrough","description":"Follow three fictional harbor reports through source tracing, identity review, publication, an interrupted answer, and portable export.","teaser":"One witness sees movement. A later report repeats the account. A third describes a stationary light. Try answering a question without losing the disagreement."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"guide/walkthrough.md","filePath":"guide/walkthrough.md"}'),l={name:"guide/walkthrough.md"};function d(p,e,u,m,b,v){const a=n("LabArticleHeader");return i(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"one-incident-several-accounts",tabindex:"-1"},[s("One incident. Several accounts. "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#one-incident-several-accounts","aria-label":'Permalink to "One incident. Several accounts."'},"​")],-1)),h(a),e[1]||(e[1]=c('<p>This is a fictional paper exercise. You can do the evidence review with three index cards. It describes how a future Ordo application should behave.</p><p><a href="/knowledge-lab/kits/three-report-source-check.txt">Get the ten-minute source-check exercise (.txt)</a>. It includes the three reports, line IDs to cite, and discussion notes you can fold away until the end.</p><h2 id="_1-import-three-reports" tabindex="-1">1. Import three reports <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-import-three-reports" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Import three reports&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On card A, a harbor witness describes a light moving over the water. Card B is a later report quoting A. On card C, another witness describes a stationary light at roughly the same time. Two reports use different names for the incident. Two people share the name Alex Lee.</p><p>Preserve each acquisition and source version. Identical bytes can share an artifact without erasing where each copy was obtained.</p><h2 id="_2-review-the-proposals" tabindex="-1">2. Review the proposals <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-review-the-proposals" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Review the proposals&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Underline the exact words about movement on each card. Draw a line from B to A and write “quotes.” Before joining the two people named Alex Lee, identify what evidence would justify that decision.</p><p>Approve the incident alias only when context supports it. Keep the two people separate unless evidence establishes identity. Record the copied report&#39;s dependence. Preserve both movement descriptions instead of averaging them into a new story.</p><h2 id="_3-publish-a-revision" tabindex="-1">3. Publish a revision <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-publish-a-revision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Publish a revision&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Review an immutable proposal against a known base revision. Publication validates identity assignments, evidence references, rights and permissions, then commits changes and their event together.</p><p>A later source or changed proposal requires a new review. No half-published result should survive a failed transaction.</p><h2 id="_4-ask-a-research-question" tabindex="-1">4. Ask a research question <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-ask-a-research-question" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Ask a research question&quot;">​</a></h2><blockquote><p>Which independent accounts describe movement, and where do their descriptions conflict?</p></blockquote><p>Chat retrieves authorized evidence from one published revision. Its answer streams with citations opening exact passages. The evidence panel identifies source dependence and unresolved assessments.</p><p>A defensible answer would attribute movement to the witness on A, note that B repeats A, and quote the stationary observation from C. The reports supply no explanation for the discrepancy.</p><h2 id="_5-test-an-interruption" tabindex="-1">5. Test an interruption <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-test-an-interruption" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. Test an interruption&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Stop a run or disconnect the browser. Committed partial text and the recorded outcome remain inspectable. Reconnection does not silently start another paid generation.</p><p>Reimport the same material: accepted records should not multiply. Correct a mistaken merge: the earlier decision should remain auditable.</p><h2 id="_6-move-the-library" tabindex="-1">6. Move the library <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-move-the-library" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Move the library&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Export the approved revision and open it on Linux without the Mac worker. Evidence remains readable. Missing capabilities are reported; unavailable semantic query encoding produces a clearly narrower search mode.</p><h2 id="what-students-should-explain" tabindex="-1">What students should explain <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-students-should-explain" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What students should explain&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Hand a classmate your three cards. Can they trace a statement to its source, count independent observations, and name one question the evidence leaves open? Then ask an engineering question: what should still be visible if the application crashes halfway through publishing your review?</p><p>Use the <a href="./../project/acceptance">requirements</a> for acceptance criteria and the <a href="./../engineering/examples">executable labs</a> for runnable, narrower contracts.</p>',23))])}const g=r(l,[["render",d]]);export{f as __pageData,g as default};
